  "case": {
    "presentation": "52-year-old woman who just established care endorses persistent fatigue over the past few months. She was previously found to be iron deficient but has not responded to intermittent oral iron supplementation. Most recent labs show anemia (Hgb 7.3 g/dL, MCV 67 fL), low ferritin (<5 ng/mL), and low iron saturation. Peripheral smear shows hypochromia and microcytosis. Only medications are oral iron and intermittent pyridium for recurrent urinary symptoms. Otherwise healthy without notable family history. Does this patient need work-up for an underlying hemoglobinopathy?",
    "recommendations": "The patient needs urgent referral to a hematologist and approximately 2.5 grams of intravenous iron. Oral iron can be continued, but is insufficient and poorly absorbed. It may also mask dark stools from a GI bleed. Additional evaluations should include vitamin B12 and methylmalonic acid levels, Helicobacter pylori testing, celiac panel, and gastrointestinal workup to rule out bleeding or malabsorption. A hemoglobinopathy evaluation is not indicated now. Stop pyridium, if used, and check urinalysis with microscopic analysis and urine hemosiderin. Refer to gastroenterology for upper and lower endoscopic evaluation."
  },
